    "reading": {
        "body": "ServiceNow's $13.3 billion revenue base funds two product lines—AI Governance and Now Assist—both embedded in enterprise workflow software rather than sold as separate tools.\n\nThe architecture matters. AI Governance operates as inventory and control within existing ServiceNow deployments, letting enterprises track, score, and approve AI systems already running on their infrastructure. Now Assist automates IT and HR processes directly. Neither requires customers to adopt new platforms. This locks AI governance into the business applications layer, dependent on ServiceNow's installed base and their existing compute allocation for core operations.\n\nThe governance product signals where enterprise AI risk has become material enough to demand tooling. Model inventories and approval workflows suggest customers now deploy AI across enough systems to lose track of it otherwise. Control Tower's expansion in June 2026 to \"discover, observe, govern, secure, and measure\" AI across any system indicates the problem is spreading beyond ServiceNow's own products.\n\nThis data does not measure how many ServiceNow customers actually use these AI features, their adoption rate, or how much revenue they represent. It does not distinguish between governance as a compliance checkbox versus genuine audit and control. It also does not reveal whether enterprise AI governance demand translates to demand for compute, buildings, or power—that depends on where and how the AI systems themselves run.",
